Origin: EmberBSD.

Linux 6.18 DRM core next to the Linux 5.6 core
==============================================

A kernel has one DRM core: the 5.6 one (attribute drmkms, files in dist/, own
code in drm/) or the 6.18 one (attribute drmkms618, files in dist618/, own code
in drm618/).  Both export the same drm_* symbols with different layouts.  Both
use the same Linux compatibility layer (attributes drmkms_linux, drmkms_i2c;
directories linux/, include/linux, include/asm), so work on the layer serves
both cores.

	dist618/	Linux 6.18.x files, MIT or "GPL-2.0 OR MIT" only, byte for
			byte as upstream (MANIFEST.tsv: sha256, licence class,
			upstream path; LINUX_VERSION)
	drm618/		files.drmkms618, own headers (include/), own rewrites of
			the GPL-only files, gpl-only.tsv, tools/
	prepare-import618.sh	(re)import of dist618, "--verify" checks it

drm618/gpl-only.tsv lists what is not imported and why: GPL-only files, files
without a licence text, uapi headers under "GPL-2.0 WITH Linux-syscall-note".
The last column names the existing BSD rewrite in drm/ (a Linux 5.6 level one
that has to be brought to 6.18), or "-" when there is none.

Selecting a core
----------------
files.drmkms618 is included from sys/conf/files, but only as a definition of
the attribute drmkms618: no default kernel configuration selects it.  The
tested way to run the 6.18 core is the loadable module sys/modules/drmkms618
(with drmkms618_sched and drmkms618_ttm) in a kernel without the 5.6 core:
configuration GENERIC_ARL_DRM618 (the ARL configuration without the 5.6
drivers).  A kernel with the attribute selected and the core built in has not
been built or tested.
Selecting both cores fails the build on purpose: drm618_exclusive.c is built
for "drmkms618 & drmkms" and contains an #error.  At run time a module of one
core cannot load into a kernel with the other: the module loader refuses
symbols that already exist (kobj_checksyms).  Modules get their own names
(drmkms618, ...) and depend on "drmkms_linux,sysmon_power" like drmkms; a
driver module built for 6.18 depends on "drmkms618" and is not interchangeable
with the 5.6 build of the same driver (the efidrm kernel version guard
already covers that).

Includes the 6.18 sources expect
-------------------------------
dist618 is byte for byte upstream, so what Linux include chains bring in for
it has to come from somewhere else.  Every drmkms618 file is compiled with
-include include/drm618_prefix.h (files.drmkms618; the compile map does the
same, NOPREFIX=1 leaves it out): the includes the sources expect, the renaming
of the NetBSD struct rb_node and of the names both systems define differently
(ALIGN, LIST_HEAD, mutex_init, RB_ROOT, ... see include/drm618_layer.h, chosen
by the number or the type of the arguments).  Headers that exist in the shared
layer with another meaning (linux/wait.h, rbtree.h, kthread.h,
interval_tree_generic.h) are shadowed in include/linux/; the 5.6 core never
sees them.  New names without a clash go into the shared layer.

Local changes to dist618 are made in separate commits after the pristine
import commit, so that "git diff" against the import shows exactly what
EmberBSD changed; BSD-only hooks that can live outside the Linux files go to
drm618/include (it comes first in the include path) or to the layer.

User mappings and PRIME
-----------------------
mmap(2) on a Linux file of the layer (drm618_vma.c) runs the mmap handler of the
file once and keeps the vm_area_struct it leaves as a uvm object: the pager
runs vm_ops open/close for the references (fork, split, unmap) and vm_ops->fault
for the faults, vmf_insert_page/pfn enter the page, remap_pfn_range is entered
lazily.  A mapping holds its file.  Only shared mappings exist; a handler that
needs anything else (a second remap_pfn_range range, zap of single pages)
fails with EOPNOTSUPP.  The TTM objects are not mapped through it yet:
drm_gem_ttm_mmap says EOPNOTSUPP (the uvm pager of a TTM object,
ttm_bo_vm_bsd.c, is not connected to the mapping of a file).

The dma-buf of the 6.18 core (drm618_dma_buf.c, include/linux/dma-buf.h) is a
Linux file too, with its own names (drm618_dma_buf_*): the dma-buf of the shared
layer (linux_dma_buf.c) serves the 5.6 core and has another interface.  The
dma-buf of this core: export, descriptor, import, attach, mmap, poll against
the reservation, llseek.  Not provided: the ioctls of the descriptor (ENOTTY),
kqueue (EINVAL), the DMA mapping of a table for another device
(dma_map_sgtable: EOPNOTSUPP), so a dma-buf of this core cannot be imported by
a driver that needs a mapped table.  drm_prime.c is built from dist618.

Test driver: sys/modules/vgem618 (same source as vgem, VGEM_CORE618), client
ember/tests/drm2-vgem-client.c, kernel checks drm2-drm618-gem and
drm2-drm618-dmabuf in drmkms_linux_test.

KMS drivers
-----------
A KMS driver for this core is a loadable module that depends on drmkms618 and
uses the atomic helpers of dist618 with
drm618/drm618_gem_framebuffer_helper.c (the framebuffers of ADDFB2 over GEM
objects) and drm618_gem_atomic_helper.c (the implicit fence of a plane); a
buffer is a GEM object over a shmem file as in vgem.  drm_simple_kms_helper.c
and drm_gem_shmem_helper.c are GPL-only and not here.  Without a vblank
interrupt the CRTC uses the vblank timer of drm_vblank.c
(DRM_CRTC_VBLANK_TIMER_FUNCS), which needs the hrtimer of the layer to run
periodically (hrtimer_forward_now and HRTIMER_RESTART), and a driver may
leave out the wait for the vblank in its commit tail when the frame is final
when the plane update returns.  The modeset objects need idr_alloc without
idr_preload (drm_mode_object_add): the layer allocates the node itself.

A signal that ends a wait in a file operation (read of events, ioctl, open) is
EINTR, never ERESTART: the restart of a system call does not look at SA_RESTART.

Test driver for KMS: efidrm618 (outside of this tree: KMS over the UEFI GOP
framebuffer, the module of the 5.6 core brought to this one); client
ember/tests/drm2-kms-client.c on raw ioctls, ember/tests/kms-shot-compare.py
for the comparison of a QEMU screendump with the pattern the client draws.

The virtio-gpu driver (sys/modules/virtiogpu618)
------------------------------------------------
The virtio-gpu driver of Linux 6.18 (dist618/drm/virtio, thirteen MIT files,
one local patch, patches.tsv) runs on this core in a QEMU guest: the module
virtiogpu618 attaches to a virtio(4) device of type 16 (the device is called
virtiogpudrm; the kernel configuration must not have viogpu or the 5.6
virtiodrm: GENERIC_ARL_DRM618 has none).  What it needs from the layer and
how it is provided:

 - Scatter lists as Linux defines them (include/linux/scatterlist.h shadow,
   drm618_scatterlist.c): an array of entries, one per run of physically
   contiguous pages, bus address and length after a mapping.  The shared
   layer's one-entry table is read under other names and never meets this
   one.  Tables carry the physical address as the bus address (no IOMMU);
   dma_map_sgtable stays EOPNOTSUPP, so a dma-buf of another device cannot be
   imported.  PAGE_MASK is not used (opposite meanings in NetBSD and in the
   layer's <linux/mm.h>).  is_vmalloc_addr says no on this core: the heap is
   walked by pages.
 - drm_gem_shmem_helper (drm618_gem_shmem_helper.c) and the encoder of
   drm_simple_kms_helper (drm618_simple_kms.c), own code: create, free, pin,
   vmap, mmap, sg table; no madvise, no import of an sg table, no dumb
   buffers, no write-combined mappings.
 - The Linux virtio device interface (drm618_virtio.c, linux/virtio*.h): a
   driver registry, probe from the attach with the features of the driver's
   table, queues as handles, commands as scatter lists (every physically
   contiguous piece becomes a DMA map of its own), reset, unused buffers,
   the virtio dma-buf operations.  Not provided: shared memory regions of a
   device (host visible blobs; drm618_virtio_vram.c refuses them with a
   message), queue contexts, interrupt affinity.  A command that waits more
   than five seconds is named in the log once a second; after two seconds the
   device is told again.  sysctl hw.virtiogpudrm: drop_notify (fault
   injection) and read only counters of commands and DMA maps.
 - Linux formatting of the log (drm618_printf.c): %pV and %ps for drm_print.c,
   without which the DRM messages were unreadable.

2D works; virgl, blob resources, context init and EXECBUFFER compile as in
Linux but were not run: the device is given no such feature in the tested
set-up.  The upstream driver does not check a framebuffer against the size of
its object and takes any cursor image up to the cursor size; the client
(ember/tests/drm2-kms-client.c) has the expectations of both drivers.

Unloading
---------
drmkms618 refuses to unload (EBUSY, before anything is taken down) while a
user mapping, a dma-buf, a sync file or a reserved file descriptor exists:
their operations live in the module text.  The character device of the core
is unregistered by the core's own exit and checked after it.
virtiogpu618 refuses while its DRM device has clients, in the unload command
and in the detach of the device (a forced detach cannot be refused; drvctl -d
does not reach virtio children, the kernel says ENOTSUP).  Files of other
kinds made by anon_inode_getfile are not counted, and between the check and
the teardown a new open is possible: unloading is for root on a machine
without clients.

Effect on the 5.6 core
----------------------
The series changes the shared layer (sys/external/bsd/drm2/linux,
.../include/linux, common/linux), so the kernel with the 5.6 core, i915,
radeon, amdgpu, nouveau and vmwgfx behave differently in these points:

 - dma_fence_is_later: for equal sequence numbers the answer is now "no" (as
   in Linux; it was "yes").  Users: amdgpu_ids.c, amdgpu_sync.c, drm_gem.c,
   ttm_bo_util.c, linux_dma_fence_chain.c.
 - dma_resv: the storage has a tagged list (usage) besides the exclusive
   fence and the shared list.  dma_resv_test_signaled_rcu and
   dma_resv_wait_timeout_rcu with shared=true now also wait for the exclusive
   fence when shared fences exist (longer waits are possible); dma_resv_copy_fences
   does not copy fences that have signalled and takes a non-const source;
   dma_resv_do_poll looks at every fence of the object.  dma_resv_add_*_fence
   never allocate and never sleep (TTM calls them under a spin lock); room is
   made in dma_resv_reserve_shared and dma_resv_reserve_fences, and a fence
   added without a reservation needs a non-sleeping allocation or the kernel
   panics rather than lose it.
 - ww_mutex_lock with an acquire context can return -EDEADLK from the wait
   (the Linux contract); callers of the 5.6 core were not all re-read.
 - idr_alloc without idr_preload allocates itself (sleeping when __GFP_WAIT
   is set): a caller that used to get -ENOMEM under a spin lock now may sleep
   there.  xa_store returns the previous entry; xa_load without the lock is
   valid only while the xarray lock is an IPL_VM spin mutex.
 - hrtimer: periodic timers (hrtimer_forward_now, HRTIMER_RESTART) work and
   HRTIMER_MODE_REL_PINNED no longer panics.
 - common/linux/linux_work.c: delayed_work is larger.

These structures have another size or layout than before: struct hrtimer,
struct delayed_work, struct dma_resv, struct xarray, struct pci_dev,
struct kref (a union now), struct hdr_sink_metadata (hdmi_type1).  The kernel
and ALL modules of the 5.6 core and of the shared layer (drmkms, drmkms_linux,
drmkms_ttm, i915drmkms, radeon, amdgpu, nouveau, vmwgfx, vgem, efidrm, ...)
must be rebuilt together; an old module with a new kernel corrupts memory
instead of failing to load.  The 5.6 core with real graphics (i915, amdgpu)
is built but has not been run on hardware with these changes: only vgem and
the old efidrm ran on it in a VM (the layer tests do not load on that kernel).

Limits of the virtio-gpu driver, fault injection, import
--------------------------------------------------------
Memory.  The pages of a GEM object (shmem file of drm618_shmem.c) are wired
and there is no swap.  An object larger than half of the physical memory is
refused when it is made (ENOMEM), and a page that is not there yet is not
taken when the free memory is down to the reserve, a sixteenth of the memory
(at least 16 MiB): the pin fails with ENOMEM and the pages already taken go
back, where it used to wait for ever until the system killed init and sshd.
This is in the GEM shmem objects of the 6.18 core (drm618_shmem.c), so
vgem618, efidrm618 and virtio-gpu have it.  It does NOT cover the TTM page
pool (ttm_pool_bsd.c): uvm_pglistalloc there still waits without a reserve,
so the first driver on the 6.18 TTM needs the same check.  A user mapping
fault that finds no memory waits and tries again, but a pending signal ends
it with SIGBUS.  The reserve and the half are a choice, not a measurement:
the machine must stay usable for its own processes, and one object of more
than half of the memory cannot be mapped and used besides.  There is no
limit per user or process and no RLIMIT_MEMLOCK accounting: whoever may open
a DRM node may wire memory up to the reserve, so the nodes are trusted
(device node permissions decide).

Size.  The ring of the device takes a command of at most as many pieces as it
has entries (the native ring asserts it), and the list of backing pages of an
object is 16 bytes per page.  The layer sets, per virtio-gpu device, the
largest object it can take in one command: (entries - 6) MiB (58 MiB for the
64 entries of QEMU's control queue); larger objects are refused at their
creation with EFBIG, before memory is taken (drm618_gem_set_limit).  A
command that cannot be queued any more is an error with a line in the log, but
the driver does not pass it on to its ioctl, hence the limit.

Fault injection.  A build for the tests only: nbmake -C sys/modules/virtiogpu618
VIRTIO_TEST=1 defines DRM618_VIRTIO_FAULT_INJECT and DRM618_VIRTIO_STATS:
sysctl hw.virtiogpudrm.drop_notify (the device is not told of commands, so it
never answers) and read only counters of commands and DMA maps.  The ordinary
build has neither the flag nor a sysctl node.  A command that waits more than
five seconds is named in the log with a growing interval (1, 2, 4 ... 60 s),
one line says when the device answers again; after two seconds the device is
told again.  Known limitation, as with Linux: a process that waits in the
driver for a device that does not answer cannot be killed until it does.

Not changed, as upstream: RESOURCE_CREATE of 0x0 is taken, a cursor image
larger than its buffer is taken.  Not done: hotplug of the display size,
virgl and 3D, a pixel check of the cursor.

Import.  select618.py selects drivers/gpu/drm/virtio; a full re-import
(sh prepare-import618.sh LINUX_TREE REFDIR, from the top of the tree) leaves
the tree unchanged except that the five files of patches.tsv are pristine
again (git diff shows exactly their local patches: apply them as before) and
that drm618/gpl-only.tsv gains the rows of the two files that are not
imported, virtgpu_vram.c and virtgpu_trace.h.  prepare-import618.sh --verify
exits 1 when patches.tsv names a file that is not imported.

